WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.660
Cut a nerve in your arm, and its axons can grow back.

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.760
Injure your spinal cord, and they mostly do not.

3
00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:11.660
Why?

4
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:16.100
A cut splits each axon in two, and the two pieces have different fates.

5
00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:21.723
The piece cut off from its cell body breaks down, in a limb nerve and in the spinal

6
00:00:21.723 --> 00:00:22.723
cord alike.

7
00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:27.733
The attached piece can grow a new tip, depending on the neuron and on what surrounds

8
00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:28.733
it.

9
00:00:28.933 --> 00:00:33.233
In a mouse nerve, the cut-off piece looks normal for a day and a half to two days.

10
00:00:34.093 --> 00:00:35.093
Then it falls apart.

11
00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:40.107
Its debris is cleared quickly in a nerve, and slowly in the brain and cord.

12
00:00:41.117 --> 00:00:44.437
Meanwhile, after a delay, the attached piece may regrow.

13
00:00:45.217 --> 00:00:47.737
In people that is often about a millimetre a day.

14
00:00:48.557 --> 00:00:51.057
Or it stalls, as many central axons do.

15
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.380
A sensory neuron in a spinal ganglion offers a test.

16
00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.020
It has one nucleus and two branches.

17
00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:01.670
Injure each branch, in separate experiments.

18
00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:03.570
Which one regrows?

19
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.640
The branch in the nerve regrows.

20
00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:14.440
The branch in the cord mostly does not.

21
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.180
Same kind of neuron, different outcome.

22
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:21.140
Is it the tissue, or what each injury tells the cell body?

23
00:01:24.583 --> 00:01:29.723
Test the second. Cut the nerve branch first, and the cord branch regrows better

24
00:01:29.723 --> 00:01:32.583
later. This is the conditioning lesion.

25
00:01:33.323 --> 00:01:36.143
So the neuron's own state matters, and the tissue may too.

26
00:01:37.117 --> 00:01:38.717
So here is the one idea.

27
00:01:39.577 --> 00:01:40.697
One cut, two fates.

28
00:01:41.477 --> 00:01:44.517
The cut-off piece destroys itself, by a program it carries.

29
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.460
The attached piece tends to regrow where the neuron's drive to grow outweighs the

30
00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.040
resistance of the tissue around it.

31
00:01:55.383 --> 00:01:57.083
So ask two things of any cut.

32
00:01:57.923 --> 00:01:59.263
What does the cut-off piece do?

33
00:02:00.063 --> 00:02:02.143
And at the attached piece, which side wins?
